From: Holodor <holodor@transentient.github.io>
Date: Wed, 13 Aug 2026 00:05:00 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] usb: typec: mux: skip unregistered switch/mux devices in set paths

A typec_switch/typec_mux handle obtained with typec_switch_get() /
fwnode_typec_mux_get() holds a reference on the underlying sw_dev/mux_dev
struct device, but nothing invalidates the handle when the supplier driver
unbinds: typec_switch_set() happily calls sw_dev->set() into the unbound
driver's freed (devm) drvdata.

Hardware-proven on the AYN Odin 3 (2026-08-12): the pmic-glink UCSI
connector holds the wcd939x-usbss orientation switch; the device link that
would normally tie their lifetimes failed to create at boot ("Failed to
create device link (0x180) with supplier 2-000e" — connector <-> usbss
fw_devlink dependency cycle).  After the usbss re-probed, the first
connector-change event dispatched through the stale handle:

  Unable to handle kernel NULL pointer dereference at 0000000000000020
  pc : regmap_update_bits_base+0x30/0x90
  lr : wcd939x_usbss_set_hsj_route+0x3c/0x190
  ...
  typec_switch_set+0x68/0xb0
  typec_set_orientation+0x28/0x80
  pmic_glink_ucsi_connector_status+0x64/0xa0
  ucsi_handle_connector_change+0xa8/0x570

The dead UCSI worker then wedged the pmic-glink pipeline for the rest of
the boot (battery gauge -110 timeouts, audio DSP graph failures).

Guard the dispatch loops: an unregistered sw_dev/mux_dev is skipped.  This
does not fix the consumer's handle going permanently quiet after a supplier
re-probe (a separate lifetime problem), but it turns a kernel oops plus
platform-wide glink wedge into a silent no-op.
---
--- a/drivers/usb/typec/mux.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/typec/mux.c
@@ -225,6 +225,13 @@ int typec_switch_set(struct typec_switch *sw,
 	for (i = 0; i < sw->num_sw_devs; i++) {
 		sw_dev = sw->sw_devs[i];

+		/*
+		 * The supplier may have unbound (and re-registered a new
+		 * sw_dev) while we hold this handle; its drvdata is gone.
+		 */
+		if (!device_is_registered(&sw_dev->dev))
+			continue;
+
 		ret = sw_dev->set(sw_dev, orientation);
 		if (ret && ret != -EOPNOTSUPP)
 			return ret;
@@ -401,6 +408,13 @@ int typec_mux_set(struct typec_mux *mux, struct typec_mux_state *state)
 	for (i = 0; i < mux->num_mux_devs; i++) {
 		mux_dev = mux->mux_devs[i];

+		/*
+		 * The supplier may have unbound (and re-registered a new
+		 * mux_dev) while we hold this handle; its drvdata is gone.
+		 */
+		if (!device_is_registered(&mux_dev->dev))
+			continue;
+
 		ret = mux_dev->set(mux_dev, state);
 		if (ret && ret != -EOPNOTSUPP)
 			return ret;
